r media and the  destroying of reputation and attempt at  destroying career  and  i'm absolutely sure that i would do it  again  and  that's not to say that it hasn't been  really really really hard and that i'm  still  not as okay as i would like to be three  years  out of the situation i certainly wish i  was doing better than i am  hill shared her advice with blasi ford i  just want to say you know to christine  it it does get better it will continue  to get better  but she really needed to hear that too  we'll get better joining us now the  podcast co-host that's salamish tillett  and cindy levy welcome to you both it  was so interesting guys to hear their  two voices together  and i can imagine what it meant to both  of them to be able to talk how did this  come about cindy um i've known professor  hill for years and recently over the  last couple of years got to meet dr ford  and i i knew that there was a  mutual admiration between the two of  them and you know they are sort of this  club of two  you know not a club that either one of  them ever necessarily would have wanted  to belong to but they're the two people  who really understand  what one another has gone through in a  way that the rest of us kind of can't  imagine yeah you point out that both of  them are very private people who didn't  want this attention and now it's turned  out to be one of the most both of them  two of the biggest testimonies public  testimonies that have been life-changing  i'm curious about what stood out to you  because christine said i just wanted to  be a good human being anita said i just  want to be as clear as possible it's not  my job to persuade they both wanted to  they say just do the right thing yeah  you it's one of the things that both  really both salamish and i noticed when  we were talking to them is this sense of  sort of civic duty and patriotism you  know they came forward simply because  they had information and you know with  both of their cases quite reluctantly i  think people forget 30 years to the day  after anita hill's testimony that you  know she very much didn't want to  testify but but was asked by the senate  judiciary committee to do so and the way  they both approached it was simply to  look at it as their job was to deliver  information and they both i think for  their own sanity had to let go of what  the outcome was going to be because it's  been 30 years we've all been listening  to anita hill's testimony  salamisha i cringe i remember watching  it very vividly and the question she was  asked are you a martyr are you a scorned  woman  speak again about the pubic hair on the  cocaine that she had already said two  times i mean i look at that and i go how  was that even allowed to to happen the  way it did yeah what are you thinking  today what does it mean to us today  today why was 16 years old when i was  watching the testimony so i didn't even  understand quite what was happening  either in terms of sexual harassment nor  the language that  she was  alleging clarence thomas used in the  workplace  but today i think one of the things  that's striking is the differences in  the experiences that  professor  hill had versus dr ford in the kind of  etiquette in the hearings  social media of course the attacks on dr  ford were differently  shown compared to professor hill but  also i think the legacy of anita hill  for all of us is to have this language  of sexual harassment and also to  understand that these aren't villains in  someone else's story these these are  three-dimensional women and one of the  things that i think was so compelling  about hearing them in conversation with  each other is that um their humanity  their believability and their their  trustworthiness was clear as day and so  when you think about the attacks that  have continuously still mounted against  them um i think their voices together  provide an alternative the legacy really  is uh illusion shattering as you put it  uh the language of sexual harassment was  not commonplace in 1991 the first survey  of sexual harassment was 1976. yeah talk  about the journey we've been on well and  by the way that was done in red book  magazine it wasn't done by the  government no one in the government was  really trying to to figure this out and  in fact the statistics are so very poor  there isn't a lot of great data about it  but we know that it's very very persua  pervasive and that probably about one in  three women and close to that of men  have experienced it in the workplace do  you think it is continued at a  consistent level or can we say that  because of conversations that began with  anita hill it's declined either one of  you take that yeah i mean i think  because of anita hill we  saw that there were shifts in  legislation and workplace protections  but the problem is that 30 years that  was kind of an immediate aftermath of  her testimony 30 years later we haven't  seen significant protections or new um  uh you know in terms of harassment in  terms of how much people can sue for  those things haven't changed so there  was an immediate bump so to speak and 30  years later we're still dealing with a  lack of enthusiasm and a lack of  critical will to deal with these issues  i remember during the anita hill case  men were relatively silent yeah do you  believe now in 2021 that we are using  our voice more when women come out yeah  i mean i would say one thing that that  is important to note is that um the  racial dynamics of the case too and the  ways in which there was a real divide  amongst african-american men and women  in response to anita hill's testimony  but uh at the time and we talked about  this in our second episode a group of  black women took out an ad in the new  york times african-american women in  defense of ourselves also in defense of  anita hill a speed up to christine  blasey ford  a group of men an interracial group of  men intergenerational group of men took  out a similar ad in new york times in  support of dr ford so i would say that's  lots of progress um and anita hill talks  about that in our that episode as  something that what a what a way to  think about a black woman's legacy that  these men are have been so moved by her  voice or black woman's voice and now  they're coalescing around these issues  on race and on gender and it also talks  about how it's not the work of one  generation to solve all these problems  it's going to be multi-generational yeah  the conversation continues it does yeah  uh the podcast is because of anita uh  salamisha tillett uh cindy levy thank  you very much thank you

